  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="transcription" xml:id="transcription_aad_0186" xml:lang="en">
          <div type="letter">
        <pb facs="https://iiif.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/aad/aad_00560/" ed="1r" type="sheet"/>
            <div type="letter_message" hand="#type_black_mendelson">
              <opener>
                <seg>
                  <placeName>
                    <lb/>123 York Street
                    <lb/>New Haven, Conn. 06511
                    <lb/>U. S. A.
                  </placeName>
                </seg>
                <dateline>
                  <date>
                    <lb/>September 10, 1973
                  </date>
                </dateline>
                <salute>
                  <lb/>Dear <rs type="person" ref="#amp_person_1">Wystan</rs>,
                </salute>
              </opener>
              <p>
                <lb/>On the Oxford Book of Light Verse, of course my answer is Yes. I only
                <lb/>hope that I can someday deserve the honor. Fortunately there are many
                <lb/>friends here who can help me across the vast wastes of my ignorance,
                <lb/>and I expect that much of the practical labor of searching and copying
                <lb/>can be done more easily in the Yale library than elsewhere.
              </p>
              <p>
                <lb/>I hope you will let me take a modest role in the collaboration--one
                <lb/>limited, for the most part, to bringing to your attentio some excellent
                <lb/>light verse that might go into the new edition. The conception of the
                <lb/>first edition is so excellent, and so characteristically your own,
                <lb/>that I should not like to tinker with it myself. Of course, I would
                <lb/>also gladly take on the job of preparing the final selection for the
                <lb/>printers. (My first candidate for inclusion in the new edition is
                <lb/>James Russell Lowell's "The Origin of Didactic Poetry.")
              </p>
              <p>
                <lb/>May I also put off much of the work until at least next Summer?
                <lb/>I shall begin looking for poems immediately, but this semester is
                <lb/>a very busy one, and, although I may be on leave next Spring, I have
                <lb/>to finish a critical book if I hope to have enough money to eat three
                <lb/>years from now. If this is too much of a dely I can of course withdraw
                <lb/>entirely.
              </p>
              <p>
                <lb/>Until we can get together, may I simply send you occasional lists of
                <lb/>possible additions and changes, together with xerox copies of poems
                <lb/>that I think you may not have seen? And please tell me anything else
                <lb/>you might like me to do in the meantime.
              </p>
              <p>
                <lb/>I can't imagine any work more enjoyable than putting toethr an anthology
                <lb/>of light verse. I am as grateful for the task as for the honor.
              </p>
              <closer hand="#hand_black_mendelson">
                <salute>
                  <lb/>Yours ever,
                </salute>
                <signed>
                  <lb/><rs type="person" ref="#amp_person_89">Edward</rs>
                </signed>
              </closer>
              <postscript>
                <ab hand="#hand_black_mendelson">
                  <lb/>P.S. Please give my good wishes to Chester, whose sausage and
                  <lb/>cabbage still move me to awe.
                </ab>
              </postscript>
            </div>
        <pb facs="https://iiif.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/aad/aad_00561/" ed="1v" type="sheet"/>
            <div>
              <ab hand="#hand_blue_unident_0186">
                <lb/>60
              </ab>
            </div>
          </div>
      </div>
